I recorded those two rules in the decision log and the daily log. No code changed, and I left CLAUDE.md alone.

I added one caution to the log entry that the rules don't cover. PowerShell saves typed commands in a history file on disk. If you type `$env:JIRA_TOKEN = "the-real-token"`, the token ends up in that file in plain text, even though the variable disappears when the window closes. Two ways to avoid it:

1. Turn off history saving for the window before you type anything:

```bash
Set-PSReadLineOption -HistorySaveStyle SaveNothing
```

2. Or read the token without typing it as a literal. This prompts with hidden input, and works in Windows PowerShell 5.1 and PowerShell 7:

```bash
$env:JIRA_TOKEN = [System.Net.NetworkCredential]::new('', (Read-Host 'Jira token' -AsSecureString)).Password
```

Repeat the second line with `CONFLUENCE_TOKEN` and the other prompt text. Don't paste a token into this chat or into a file.

